00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:00.870 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in february congress grilled executives  from robin hood and citadel securities  over their role in the gamestop trading  frenzy  and throughout the hearing a phrase came  up over and over again  payment per order flow payment for order  flow payment for overflow payment for  order flow  with respect to payment for order flow  we simply  play by the rules the road payment for  order flow is a decade's old practice  that's at the heart of how commission  free brokers  operate and it's creating controversy  critics claim that the practice creates  a conflict of interest  that is bad for investors while it may  enable free commissions and explicit  costs  there are implied costs we feel everyone  ignores  but supporters say the practice is  misunderstood and that it's actually  good for investors whether you're a  robin hood client or a fidelity client  you're getting a price that is at or  better than the national best bit of  best offer  right that's the thing there's nothing  nefarious going on here following the  gamestop episode  the sec is expected to conduct a review  that could change the rules of payment  for order flow  and how brokers operate  [Music]  when an investor buys a stock on a  commission free trading app the  transaction is executed  almost instantly but a lot is going on  behind the scenes  let's take a look at the main players  there are buyers the investors who want  to purchase a stock  and there are sellers the investors who  want to unload a stock  there's also the broker the firm that  accepts investors orders and makes sure  they get executed  and finally there are the market makers  which buy the orders from the broker and  execute them these are usually high  frequency traders like virtue financial  and citadel securities and they are the  players that make the trade actually  happen  when you're the investor and you're  buying the market maker's selling  if you're the investor and you're  selling the market maker is buying  but the market maker hopes to do a lot  of little trades all day long  and eventually arrive at a place where  they're flat  zero commission brokers make money by  selling customers orders to market  makers  this is payment for order flow the  practice enables trades to be executed  at a high volume  very quickly it also means that most  orders entered by small investors never  get sent to public exchanges  like the new york stock exchange or  nasdaq instead small investors orders  get executed privately by market makers  let's break down how the process works  say an investor wants to sell 200 shares  of a stock  so they place an order with a broker the  chances that another person wants to buy  that exact amount of shares at the same  time  is pretty low but that's okay because  the broker  routes the orders to a market maker the  market maker fills it within a fraction  of a second  but that comes with risk the risk that  the price of the stock could fall before  the market maker can sell the 200 shares  to someone else  the market maker is paid for taking on  this risk through what is known as the  bid ask spread  you can see it here the spread is the  difference in price between what buyers  are paying for a stock  and what sellers are selling for it  doesn't usually amount to much  on stock exchanges it's often just a  penny per share  when market makers execute trades  privately the spread can be  even tighter this is because the prices  can be in fractions of a penny  say using our example that the spread is  just half a cent  so buying and selling those 200 shares  would make the market maker  one dollar but as millions of shares are  traded the profits from the spread add  up  it's easy money for them the retail  investors are small they don't place  very large orders and they don't tend to  be  extremely well informed about the  movements of the stock price  in 2020 these brokerages collected  almost 2.6 billion dollars in payments  for stock and option orders  and this is part of what's driving the  current controversy over the practice  some critics say that payment for order  flow creates a conflict of interest  between the broker's duty to seek the  best execution for trades  and its desire to maximize its own  profits  in the u.s brokers have what is called a  duty of best execution  they have to get you the best possible  execution on your trades or at least  attempt to do so if a broker is taking a  lot of money and payment for order  overflow  then odds are it's not really giving you  a best execution  critics say this is because brokerages  could be tempted to send customer orders  to the market centers  that pay them the biggest rebate or that  brokers could be demanding greater  payments from the market makers  this is why the practice is banned in  some other countries like the uk  in other countries where payment for  flow is illegal  everything has to go to exchanges so  everything is in the  lit markets where everybody can see the  price is coming together  all the trading happens kind of out in  the open  some critics even worry that market  makers could be front-running investor  stock trades  but supporters say these claims aren't  true brokers and market makers argue  that payment for order flow often gives  investors a better price than the best  market price  and that it helps make trading easier so  payment forward flow has a very big  benefit for investors  and that is it has allowed the emergence  of zero commission trading  it was just a few years ago that brokers  were typically charging  495 795 or more for a single stock trade  a couple years ago that went away robin  hood set the practice  and then a bunch of others like schwab  td ameritrade  e-trade followed a robin hood  spokesperson said that robin hood sends  customer orders to market makers that  typically offer better prices than  public exchanges and that it's proud of  what it's done to change the industry  for all retail investors  in the past the sec has approved of  payment for order flow as long as  brokers make certain disclosures  like where they're routing investors  orders and how much they're getting paid  for them  the agency has said that small investors  generally get better prices when their  orders are sent to market makers  rather than exchanges but the volatility  in gamestop is raising alarms in  congress where lawmakers are calling for  fresh scrutiny of payment for order flow  in february sec acting chairwoman alison  herron lee wrote a letter showing  support for a wholesale review of the  practice  if the review uncovers any sort of  abuses what i think is most likely going  to happen is that the sec  will require some sort of new  disclosures coming out of brokers  and maybe out of market makers if  payment forward flow were banned  market makers would still be able to  make money after all  they're still doing the same thing  collecting the bid ask spread  however the thing that would likely  change is that zero commission trading  would be endangered as the gamestop saga  continues to unfold  the future of payment for order flow and  zero commission trading  remains uncertain

